/-!
# The first two basic orbits of the second Janko group

The degree-100 ATLAS model `TauCeti.Sporadic.J2` is transitive, and the orbit of `1` under
its stabilizer of `0` has length `63`. These give the first two factors in a stabilizer-chain
order computation: its order is `6300` times the order of its two-point stabilizer.

Explicit words in the two ATLAS generators witness both orbit lower bounds. For the upper
bound on the second orbit, a binary relation on the 100 points is checked to be invariant under
both generators. Its row at `0` has exactly 63 entries. The relation and the word tables are
private computation data; all computations are checked by the kernel.

## References

* R. A. Wilson, R. A. Parker, J. N. Bray et al., *ATLAS of Finite Group Representations*,
  version 3, representation `J2G1-p100B0`, <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/>.
* Á. Seress, *Permutation Group Algorithms*, Cambridge Tracts in Mathematics 152,
  Cambridge University Press, 2003, §4.1 (basic orbits and the order formula).
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti.Sporadic.J2

open MulAction

/-- The ordered points `0, 1, 2, …`, reduced modulo 100, used for the stabilizer chain. -/
def basePoint (i : ℕ) : Fin 100 := Fin.ofNat 100 i

/-- The stabilizer-chain base is the natural-number sequence reduced modulo 100. -/
theorem basePoint_def (i : ℕ) : basePoint i = Fin.ofNat 100 i := (rfl)

/-! ### Explicit orbit witnesses -/

private def word (w : List (Fin 2)) : Equiv.Perm (Fin 100) :=
  (w.map fun i ↦ if i = 0 then a else b).prod

private theorem word_mem (w : List (Fin 2)) : word w ∈ J2 := by
  apply list_prod_mem
  simp only [List.mem_map]
  rintro _ ⟨i, _, rfl⟩
  split
  · exact a_mem
  · exact b_mem

